export anchor DomainCheck:
    question:
        A domain check answers one question. Is the .com domain that
        matches this name free to register, already registered, or not
        known right now.
    rule:
        Only a live lookup may report available or taken. A lookup that
        fails, times out or is rate limited reports unknown. Unknown is
        never shown as available and never shown as taken.
    results:
        - unchecked, the name has not been looked up
        - available, the lookup says the domain is free
        - taken, the lookup says the domain is registered
        - unknown, no answer is known right now, with the reason shown to the person
        - not valid, the name cannot form a valid .com domain, with the reason in plain words
    notValid:
        A name that breaks the spelling rule in a way that no repair
        settles, such as one that becomes empty or longer than a domain may
        be, is shown as not valid. The message says what is wrong in words a
        person understands, such as a name that is too long or that has
        nothing left once its punctuation is removed. A not-valid name is
        never shown as available, taken or unknown, and no lookup runs for
        it, so it spends no check from the allowance. The other names in the
        shortlist are checked as usual. A name that can be repaired by the
        spelling rule is repaired and looked up, never shown as not valid.
    spelling:
        The domain is built from the name by one fixed rule. The name is
        put in lower case, spaces and punctuation are removed, letters
        outside basic Latin are transliterated or dropped, and .com is
        added. The same name always gives the same domain, and the result
        must be a valid domain name.
    freshness:
        Every result is shown with the time of its check. A result older
        than the configured freshness window is marked stale and is
        checked again before the person relies on it.
    bulk:
        A person can check a whole shortlist in one action. Each name
        keeps its own outcome, and a partial failure never hides the names
        that did get an answer.
    service:
        The lookup source is chosen by the operator and is not part of the
        promise to the person. The product ships with a lookup chain that
        needs no subscription, so a check costs nothing to run.
    fastestPath:
        A check stops at the first answer that settles the question. The
        order is chosen by speed, and the cheapest and quickest step runs
        first.
    steps:
        - A DNS query for the name servers of the domain. Name servers present means the domain is registered, and this step is the end of the check
        - An RDAP query over HTTPS when the DNS query found no name servers. A not-found reply means the domain is free, and any other successful reply means it is registered
        - A WHOIS query only when RDAP is missing or fails for that domain
        - A paid lookup service only when the operator has configured one, or when every step above failed
    whyTwoSteps:
        A domain can be registered and still have no name servers. So a
        DNS query alone may prove a name taken but never proves it free.
        Only the structured registration query may report available.
    confirmation:
        One source is enough to report available, but it is never taken
        from a stored result alone. Before available is shown, the
        registration query runs again on the spot, and the name is shown as
        available only when that fresh reply is a not-found reply.
    doubt:
        Any doubt makes the result unknown, never available. A reply that
        is late, that is not the expected structured shape, that comes from
        a route with no registration query, or that another source
        contradicts is unknown, with the reason shown to the person.
    takenIsCheaper:
        A taken result is accepted from any step in the chain, because a
        later step can only make a taken answer stronger. The extra care is
        spent on the answer that costs the person something if it is wrong.
    cacheReuse:
        A stored available result may stand in for the first step, but
        never for the fresh confirmation, so the extra query runs for every
        name shown as available.
    registrationQuery:
        RDAP is a standard HTTP and JSON form of registration data. It is
        preferred over WHOIS because it answers in one shape for every
        registry, is not rate limited per client address like WHOIS, and
        runs over ordinary HTTPS that a hosted product can pool and cache.
        The .com registry publishes its own RDAP service, which is the
        fastest reliable route for the one domain the product checks.
    registries:
        The list of top level domains comes from the public root zone list
        published by the internet address registry, refreshed daily by the
        product and never typed in by hand. The addresses of each
        registry's registration service come from the public bootstrap file
        for that protocol, not from a list maintained here. A registry that
        is missing from the bootstrap file falls through to the next step.
    scope:
        The first version checks .com only. The lookup chain is written
        against the registry list rather than against .com, so adding a
        top level domain is a setting change and not new code.
    cache:
        A settled answer is stored per domain, shared by every person, and
        served within one freshness window instead of looked up again. A
        shortlist that maps two names to the same domain costs one lookup.
        Names already checked are not checked again inside the window. A
        stored available result is still confirmed afresh, as cacheReuse
        says.
    budget:
        A single check gives up after a short fixed time and reports
        unknown. A shortlist check runs its names in parallel with a cap on
        how many lookups run at once, so one slow registry cannot slow the
        whole list or exhaust the product's connections.
    performance:
        A single name check answers within a few seconds. A shortlist
        check runs the names in parallel and shows each result as it
        arrives, so a slow name does not hold back the rest.
    noSideEffects:
        A domain check never buys, reserves or watches a domain. It only
        reads public registration data.
